# Path App architecture

**First milestone:** An organizer uploads one real phone scan, chooses **Unlisted**, copies the link, and a teammate opens and orbits the scan on a second phone while signed out. Record the file size and time to first interactive view. A successful download proves transport. The second-phone test proves the user experience.

## System and ownership

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Phone["Phone capture / exported scan"] -->|upload| API["Sam · API"]
    Web["Arielle · organizer website"] -->|upload or change sharing| API
    API --> DB["Scan metadata and viewer grants"]
    API --> Store["Private object storage"]
    Guest["Guest browser /s/:shareId"] -->|resolve link| API
    API -->|authorized asset| Guest
    API -. selected frames .-> Gemini["Gemini API"]
    Gemini -. draft landmarks .-> Web
```

| Area | Owner | First handoff |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Website](../apps/web/README.md) | Arielle | Upload a fixture and render the returned asset by opening the share URL in a fresh browser session. |
| [API and storage](../services/api/README.md) | Sam | Persist scan bytes and sharing rules. Resolve a link after a server restart. |
| [Capture and processing](../apps/capture/README.md) | Krishna and Anish | Deliver one measured phone export, its format and orientation, plus a few original frames with stable IDs for the Gemini trial. |

The account system, framework and hosting provider are open choices. A practical starting stack is React, TypeScript and Three.js for the web, a same-origin HTTP API, Postgres for ownership and grants, and private object storage for large assets. The website can proxy `/api` to Sam’s service during development. Sam can choose Auth0 if pursuing its track, or use a bundled auth, database and storage service to reduce setup. The local mock is a disposable integration tool. [Three.js loaders](./scan-formats.md) · [Supabase private buckets](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage/buckets/fundamentals) · [Prize plan](./prize-plan.md)

## Sharing

| Visibility | Appears in public discovery | Who can open the link and load the scene |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Public | Yes | Anyone, including signed-out visitors |
| Unlisted (link only) | No | Anyone who receives the opaque link, including someone it was forwarded to |
| Private (invited viewers) | No | The owner and specific signed-in accounts with a viewer grant |

Use **Private** as the proposed default until the owner chooses an audience. The owner manages grants and visibility. A private invitee can view the scan. Store the scan’s owner, an opaque share ID, visibility, asset object key, format, byte size and processing state. A unique pair of scan ID and account ID represents each private viewer grant.

Keep the bucket private for every mode. Resolve the share page, metadata, thumbnail and asset request under the current sharing rule. The public index returns ready, public scans only. When the owner switches to private or revokes a viewer, recheck access on the next request, including direct asset requests. A server-proxied asset read makes this behavior straightforward for the demo. Bytes a viewer already downloaded remain on their device.

## Upload and publication

1. Verify the uploader’s session and record a pending upload owned by that account. Accept the file into staging. For large phone exports, Sam can switch to direct storage upload and finalize with an idempotent API call.
2. Check the stored bytes, format and parseability before marking a scan ready. A retry of finalization should resolve to the same scan. A failed upload stays out of discovery and can be retried or cleaned up.
3. Return a stable website path such as `/s/<shareId>`. The website resolves the share metadata and fetches the authorized asset to render. Keeping a website URL lets the team replace the storage backend without changing shared links.
4. In the later event workflow, store a contributed section as a draft. The organizer verifies alignment and approves a published event revision. Apply access checks to all included assets so adding a private scan to a public event cannot silently expand its audience.

## After the first share works

- **Landmarks and directions.** Keep selected RGB frames and their scan-local camera poses during capture. A geometry-only export can lose the original images. Start by matching a few frames to manually verified waypoints. Gemini suggests landmark names and visual cues for organizer review. [Gemini demo design](./gemini-demo.md)
- **QR and routing.** Each entrance QR identifies an event and a known starting waypoint. Destinations reference waypoints, and hosts connect walkable edges. Compute the shortest path by edge length and handle an unreachable destination in the viewer. The fixture uses a single event frame in meters, with +Y up. [Venue fixture and axes](../fixtures/README.md)
- **Contributions.** Test with one extra section. Before approval a guest sees the original venue. After approval the guest can open the same event link and see the new section aligned to a known anchor.

## Decisions to record during the hackathon

| Decision | Who resolves it | Evidence to put in the PR |
| --- | --- | --- |
| PLY or textured GLB for the first real scan | Krishna, Anish and Arielle | Same corridor on a second phone, recognizable landmark, correct scale, file size and load time. [Phone trial](./scan-formats.md) |
| Backend, identity provider and storage | Sam | Restart the service, reload the same link, then verify identity-based private reads and revocation on the raw asset. |
| First guest experience | Arielle | A guest can open the link, see the scan, rotate it and identify the intended turn without setup. |
| Which prize claims to submit | All four | A working feature and reproducible evidence from this weekend for each selected track. [Prize plan](./prize-plan.md) |
# Backend plan

**Owner:** Sam. **Stage 1 outcome:** the synthetic T corridor can be migrated and seeded in Tiger Data PostgreSQL, then read by a later Next.js API. The actual scan upload and second-phone viewing remain the first integrated milestone.

## Architecture and contracts

Use a single Next.js App Router application with server-only route handlers. PostgreSQL on Tiger Data holds users, access grants, spaces, scans, navigation graphs, saved paths and events. Drizzle ORM and committed Drizzle Kit migrations define the schema; postgres.js connects over the supplied TLS URL. Vultr Object Storage holds private GLB/PLY and image bytes. The server issues short-lived S3 presigned URLs only after checking identity and resource access. Auth0 verifies identity; the database stores its `sub`. Gemini analyzes selected camera frames and proposes landmarks for human confirmation. It never creates coordinates or graph edges.

Spaces own one coordinate frame in metres with +Y up. Scan geometry is stored in object storage; each scan records a 16-number transform into its space. Matrix ordering and final capture format must be agreed with Krishna and Anish before real-scan integration. The current synthetic fixture uses +X right and −Z forward. Graph nodes and edges are explicit walkable data; route calculation in Arielle's viewer uses only authorized, connected spaces. A QR marker or manual selection supplies the MVP start point.

Visibility is `private`, `unlisted`, or `public`, with `private` as the default. Public resources are discoverable; unlisted resources require an opaque link; private resources require ownership, a viewer grant, or event membership where explicitly supported. A public event or path must not expose a private space or scan merely by referencing it. Every metadata, graph and object URL request must recheck effective access server-side. Existing downloaded bytes cannot be revoked. Event rows reference spaces, paths and destinations rather than copying them. Saved paths are ordered node references and may be used outside events.

The database enforces IDs, relationships and same-space graph links. Application code in later phases must enforce ownership, visibility composition, event membership, path connectivity and authorization. No hypertables are needed for the MVP; timestamped contribution history and navigation telemetry can be evaluated after the core workflow works.

## Planned HTTP contract

Use `/api/users/me`; `/api/spaces` and `/api/spaces/:id/map`; `/api/scans/upload-url`, `/api/scans`, `/api/scans/:id/download-url`; `/api/spaces/:id/graph`; `/api/destinations`; `/api/paths` and `/api/paths/:id`; `/api/events`, `/api/events/:id`, `/api/events/:id/join`; `/api/scans/:id/analyze`. Add update/delete routes when needed. Validate request bodies with Zod and return consistent JSON errors, for example `{ "error": { "code": "FORBIDDEN", "message": "..." } }`. Preserve the current mock's `shareId`, `sharePath`, and authorized `assetPath` semantics when replacing it.

## Implementation checklist

- [x] **Phase 1 — Database:** Drizzle schema, committed migration, synthetic fixture seed, connection check, database setup instructions. Live Tiger Data connectivity awaits the instance URL.
- [x] **Phase 2 — Authentication:** Auth0 SDK login shell, API audience, JWT verification, user sync and owner/event-member helpers. Real login and Tiger Data profile sync verified by Sam.
- [ ] **Phase 3 — Storage:** development-only local GLB storage, direct upload/download, scan metadata and byte validation are implemented. Private Vultr bucket and presigned transfer await service availability. See [Scan API contract](scan-api.md).
- [x] **Phase 4 — Core APIs:** Zod-validated space, graph, destination, path, event and scan operations, access checks and example requests are implemented. See [Core API contract](core-api.md) and [Scan API contract](scan-api.md).
- [ ] **Phase 5 — Integration:** real capture transform and format, 3D viewer, connected pathfinding, event sharing, second-phone test.
- [ ] **Phase 6 — Gemini:** selected-frame analysis, structured landmark suggestions, human confirmation, optional directions from verified routes.
- [ ] **Phase 7 — Deployment:** Docker on Vultr Cloud Compute, HTTPS, secrets, complete demo walkthrough.

## Existing repo and migration boundary

The original repo had no Next.js package, database client or prior migration. Stage 1 added Drizzle under `services/api/`; the Auth0 login shell now uses Next.js there as well. `tools/scan_mock.py` remains an in-memory Python reference API with `X-Dev-User` and process-local bytes, not the hosted backend. `docs/architecture.md` still describes the earlier open stack choice, including a Supabase option. The chosen stack in this plan supersedes that option. The fixture and mock remain useful for integration until the server routes replace them. The placeholder scan seed is `pending` and has no object in Vultr.
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { createHash, randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
import { mkdtemp, readFile, rm } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import path from 'node:path';
import { test } from 'node:test';
import { eq } from 'drizzle-orm';
import { connectDatabase } from '../../db/client';
import { eventMembers, events, eventSpaces, scans, scanViewers, spaces, users } from '../../db/schema';
import { LocalScanStorage } from '../storage';
import { POST as uploadRoute } from '../../app/api/spaces/[id]/scans/route';
import { GET as downloadRoute } from '../../app/api/scans/[id]/file/route';
import { boundedFormData, canReadScan, createScan, deleteScan, downloadScan, identityTransform,
  listScans, parseUpload, readScan, transform, updateScan, validateGlb } from './scans';

function sampleGlb(extra: Record<string, unknown> = {}) {
  const json = Buffer.from(JSON.stringify({ asset: { version: '2.0' }, scene: 0, scenes: [{ nodes: [] }], ...extra }));
  const paddedLength = Math.ceil(json.length / 4) * 4;
  const bytes = Buffer.alloc(20 + paddedLength, 0x20);
  bytes.write('glTF', 0);
  bytes.writeUInt32LE(2, 4);
  bytes.writeUInt32LE(bytes.length, 8);
  bytes.writeUInt32LE(paddedLength, 12);
  bytes.write('JSON', 16);
  json.copy(bytes, 20);
  return bytes;
}

test('GLB validation and multipart fields reject invalid files and transforms', () => {
  const bytes = sampleGlb();
  validateGlb(bytes, 'venue.glb');
  assert.throws(() => validateGlb(bytes, 'venue.png'));
  assert.throws(() => validateGlb(Buffer.from('not a GLB'), 'venue.glb'));
  const wrongSize = Buffer.from(bytes);
  wrongSize.writeUInt32LE(bytes.length + 4, 8);
  assert.throws(() => validateGlb(wrongSize, 'venue.glb'));
  const form = new FormData();
  form.set('file', new File([bytes], 'venue.glb'));
  form.set('name', 'Hall');
  form.set('transform', JSON.stringify(identityTransform));
  assert.equal(parseUpload(form).input.name, 'Hall');
  form.set('transform', JSON.stringify([1, 2]));
  assert.throws(() => parseUpload(form));
});

test('GLB is self-contained and alignment cannot collapse geometry or add perspective', () => {
  for (const key of ['buffers', 'images']) {
    for (const uri of ['textures/room.jpg', 'https://example.com/room.bin', '//example.com/file', 'blob:other-session']) {
      assert.throws(() => validateGlb(sampleGlb({ [key]: [{ uri }] }), 'room.glb'), /must be embedded/);
    }
    validateGlb(sampleGlb({ [key]: [{ uri: 'data:application/octet-stream;base64,AAAA' }] }), 'room.glb');
  }
  const calibrated = [...identityTransform];
  calibrated[0] = calibrated[5] = calibrated[10] = 0.00001;
  calibrated[12] = 2;
  assert.deepEqual(transform.parse(calibrated), calibrated);
  for (const [index, value] of [[0, 0], [3, 1], [7, 1], [11, 1], [15, 0], [12, Infinity]]) {
    const invalid = [...identityTransform]; invalid[index] = value;
    assert.equal(transform.safeParse(invalid).success, false);
  }
});

test('invalid bearer tokens cannot upload or download', async () => {
  const id = randomUUID();
  const headers = { Authorization: 'Bearer invalid' };
  const upload = await uploadRoute(new Request(`http://localhost/api/spaces/${id}/scans`, {
    method: 'POST', headers,
  }), { params: Promise.resolve({ id }) });
  const download = await downloadRoute(new Request(`http://localhost/api/scans/${id}/file`, {
    headers,
  }), { params: Promise.resolve({ id }) });
  assert.equal(upload.status, 401);
  assert.equal(download.status, 401);
});

test('multipart upload rejects an oversized request before parsing', async () => {
  const request = new Request('http://localhost/api/scans', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data; boundary=test', 'Content-Length': String(34 * 1024 * 1024) },
    body: 'small',
  });
  await assert.rejects(boundedFormData(request), { status: 413 });
});

